{"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C01602","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"Ornithine is an amino acid produced in the urea cycle by the splitting off of urea from arginine. It is a central part of the urea cycle, which allows for the disposal of excess nitrogen. L-Ornithine is a precursor of citrulline and arginine.","type":"literal"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C01602 + C00026 <=> C01110 + C00025","type":"literal"},"ReactionTitle":{"value":"Ornithine + 2-Oxoglutarate <=> 5-Amino-2-oxopentanoic acid + L-Glutamate","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00129","type":"uri"}},
{"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00064","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A non-essential amino acid present abundantly throughout the body and is involved in many metabolic processes. It is synthesized from glutamic acid and ammonia. It is the principal carrier of nitrogen in the body and is an important energy source for many cells. [PubChem]","type":"literal"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00582 + C00064 <=> C00010 + C04148","type":"literal"},"ReactionTitle":{"value":"Phenylacetyl-CoA + L-Glutamine <=> CoA + alpha-N-Phenylacetyl-L-glutamine","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00130","type":"uri"}},
